                <head>B. Meetings at Bonn between representatives of the Federal Republic and the
                    Allied Deputy High Commissioners to discuss certain aspects of a German
                    contribution to the defense of Western Europe, January 9–June 4, 1951</head>
                <p rendition="#center">
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </p>
                <p>On December 18 the NATO Council at its sixth session in Brussels approved a
                    decision to begin talks at Bonn between the Allied High Commission and
                    representatives of the Federal Republic of Germany concerning certain aspects of
                    a German contribution to Western defense. On the following day the Foreign
                    Ministers of the United States, the United Kingdom, and France agreed to begin
                    discussions with the Federal Republic on a new contractual relationship to
                    replace the Occupation Statute for Germany and the Charter of the Allied High
                    Commission. These decisions, together with the approval given to the projected
                    talks at Paris for a European Army, became known as the “Brussels decisions” or
                    “Brussels formula.”</p>
                <p>The documentation that follows indicates the main lines of the Bonn talks
                    (sometimes referred to as the Petersberg talks) on a possible German military
                    contribution to Western defense. For documentation on contractual relations and
                    the European Army talks, see <ref target="frus:frus1951v03p2/pg_1446">pages
                        1446</ref> ff. and <ref target="#pg755">755</ref> ff.</p>






























